how to install prometheuse,graphana,opentelmetry,loki,traces in kubernets cluster please explain for devops enginner please show production ready approach

ChatGPT said:
Love this question 😄 — this is exactly how modern production Kubernetes observability stacks are built.

I’ll explain this like a real DevOps engineer in a company, not theory.
We’ll use a production-ready, CNCF-recommended approach that most orgs follow today.

🎯 Goal: Full Observability on Kubernetes
We want 3 pillars of observability:

Pillar	Tool
Metrics	Prometheus
Logs	Loki
Traces	Tempo
Visualization	Grafana
Collection / Agent	OpenTelemetry
👉 Best production approach:
Use Grafana Alloy (or OpenTelemetry Collector) + Helm charts

🏗️ Production Architecture (Very Important)
[ Application Pods ]
   |  (metrics, logs, traces)
   v
[ OpenTelemetry Collector / Grafana Alloy ]
   |       |        |
   v       v        v
Prometheus  Loki   Tempo
      \       |      /
          Grafana
Why this is production-ready?
✅ No app vendor lock-in
✅ Easy scaling
✅ Centralized control
✅ Same setup works on AWS / Azure / GCP / on-prem

🧠 Key Design Decisions (What companies actually do)
Decision	Reason
Helm charts	Versioned, rollback, repeatable
Namespace observability	Clean separation
OTEL Collector / Alloy	Single agent for logs + metrics + traces
Grafana dashboards	Unified view
Tempo (not Jaeger)	Cloud-native tracing
📦 Step 1: Create Observability Namespace
kubectl create namespace observability
📦 Step 2: Install Prometheus (Metrics)
Use kube-prometheus-stack (industry standard)
helm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts
helm repo update
helm install prometheus \
  prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack \
  -n observability
What this gives you:
Prometheus server

Node exporter

kube-state-metrics

Alertmanager

Grafana (optional)

✅ Production ready out-of-the-box

📦 Step 3: Install Loki (Logs)
helm repo add grafana https://grafana.github.io/helm-charts
helm repo update
helm install loki grafana/loki-stack \
  -n observability \
  --set promtail.enabled=false
⚠️ We disable promtail because OTEL / Alloy will handle logs

📦 Step 4: Install Tempo (Traces)
helm install tempo grafana/tempo \
  -n observability
Tempo is:

Cheap

Scales insanely well

Used by big companies instead of Jaeger

📦 Step 5: Install Grafana (UI)
If not already installed by prometheus stack:

helm install grafana grafana/grafana \
  -n observability
Get admin password:

kubectl get secret grafana \
  -n observability \
  -o jsonpath="{.data.admin-password}" | base64 --decode
📦 Step 6: Install OpenTelemetry Collector (MOST IMPORTANT)
This is the heart of the system ❤️

helm repo add open-telemetry https://open-telemetry.github.io/opentelemetry-helm-charts
helm repo update
helm install otel-collector \
  open-telemetry/opentelemetry-collector \
  -n observability
🧩 Production OTEL Collector Config (Real World)
receivers:
  otlp:
    protocols:
      grpc:
      http:

processors:
  batch:
  memory_limiter:
    limit_mib: 512

exporters:
  prometheus:
    endpoint: "0.0.0.0:8889"

  loki:
    endpoint: "http://loki:3100/loki/api/v1/push"

  otlp/tempo:
    endpoint: "tempo:4317"
    tls:
      insecure: true

service:
  pipelines:
    metrics:
      receivers: [otlp]
      processors: [batch]
      exporters: [prometheus]

    logs:
      receivers: [otlp]
      processors: [batch]
      exporters: [loki]

    traces:
      receivers: [otlp]
      processors: [batch]
      exporters: [otlp/tempo]
👉 This single config handles everything.

📦 Step 7: Application Instrumentation (Two Ways)
✅ Option 1: ZERO Code Change (Most Companies)
Java example:

java -javaagent:opentelemetry-javaagent.jar \
     -Dotel.service.name=payment-service \
     -Dotel.exporter.otlp.endpoint=http://otel-collector:4317 \
     -jar app.jar
✔️ Metrics
✔️ Logs
✔️ Traces
✔️ No code changes

✅ Option 2: Code Instrumentation (Advanced Teams)
@WithSpan
public void processOrder() {
   // business logic
}
Used when:

Custom spans

Business metrics

🔍 Step 8: Connect Grafana Data Sources
In Grafana UI:

Type	URL
Prometheus	http://prometheus-kube-prometheus-prometheus:9090
Loki	http://loki:3100
Tempo	http://tempo:3100
Now you can:

Click metric → see logs → jump to trace 😎

