You said:
but if our app is running on another namespace like prod namespace how our application  pod will connect to opentelmetry collector then?

ChatGPT said:
Excellent question 🔥 — this is exactly the thing that confuses people in real clusters.

Short reassurance first:
👉 Namespace does NOT block connectivity by default in Kubernetes.
Pods in prod can freely talk to pods in observability as long as you use the correct Service DNS and you didn’t add NetworkPolicies.

Now let’s break it down properly, production-style.

🧠 How cross-namespace communication works in Kubernetes
Kubernetes DNS format:

<service-name>.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local
So if your OpenTelemetry Collector service is:

Service name: otel-collector
Namespace: observability
Then from ANY namespace (including prod) you can reach it at:

otel-collector.observability.svc.cluster.local
✅ This is standard production behavior

🏗️ Recommended Production Setup
Namespaces
prod             → Application pods
observability    → Prometheus, Loki, Tempo, OTEL
Traffic Flow
prod/app-pod
   |
   | OTLP (gRPC / HTTP)
   v
otel-collector.observability.svc.cluster.local:4317
✅ Step 1: Expose OTEL Collector via Service
Check service:

kubectl get svc -n observability
You should see something like:

otel-collector   ClusterIP   10.96.20.15   <none>   4317/TCP,4318/TCP
ClusterIP is PERFECT 👍
No NodePort / LoadBalancer needed inside cluster.

✅ Step 2: Application config (prod namespace)
Java / Spring Boot (NO code change)
env:
  - name: OTEL_SERVICE_NAME
    value: payment-service
  - name: OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT
    value: http://otel-collector.observability.svc.cluster.local:4317
Or JVM args:

-Dotel.exporter.otlp.endpoint=http://otel-collector.observability.svc.cluster.local:4317
That’s it.
Your app in prod → OTEL in observability.

🔍 Step 3: Validate connectivity (DevOps trick)
Exec into app pod:

kubectl exec -it app-pod -n prod -- sh
Test DNS:

nslookup otel-collector.observability.svc.cluster.local
Test port:

nc -zv otel-collector.observability.svc.cluster.local 4317
If this works → traces will flow.

🚨 What if NetworkPolicies are enabled? (REAL PRODUCTION CASE)
In many companies NetworkPolicies ARE enabled.

Then you must explicitly allow traffic.

🔐 NetworkPolicy Example (Production Safe)
Allow prod → observability (OTEL only)
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-prod-to-otel
  namespace: observability
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: otel-collector
  ingress:
    - from:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              name: prod
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 4317
Label namespace:

kubectl label namespace prod name=prod
🔐 Why companies isolate observability namespace
Reason	Explanation
Security	Logs may contain sensitive data
Stability	OTEL failure should not affect apps
Scaling	Observability scales differently
RBAC	Separate access control
🧠 Alternative Production Pattern (DaemonSet)
Some companies run OTEL as DaemonSet:

Each node:
  ├── App pods
  └── OTEL Collector (localhost:4317)
Then app config becomes:
-Dotel.exporter.otlp.endpoint=http://localhost:4317
✔️ Faster
✔️ Less network hops
✔️ Very popular in high-traffic clusters

⚠️ Common Mistakes (Seen in real incidents)
❌ Using service name without namespace
❌ Using NodePort internally
❌ Forgetting NetworkPolicy
❌ Using external LB unnecessarily

🏁 Final Mental Model (Remember this)
Namespaces are logical isolation, NOT network isolation (by default).

Communication depends on:

Service DNS

NetworkPolicies (if enabled)

