You said:
ok but how prometheuse will connect to open telemetric metric url to pull metrics???

ChatGPT said:
Ahhh 💡 this is the CORE Prometheus question — once you get this, observability finally clicks.
Let’s walk it exactly like it works in real production Kubernetes, no hand-waving.

🧠 First: Prometheus model (VERY IMPORTANT)
Prometheus is PULL-based, not push.

So Prometheus never “connects to OTEL” randomly.
It scrapes HTTP endpoints that expose metrics in Prometheus format.

So the question becomes:

Where are metrics exposed as /metrics?

🏗️ Production Pattern (What companies actually do)
Flow for metrics
Application
   |
   |  OTLP (push)
   v
OpenTelemetry Collector
   |
   |  exposes /metrics (Prom format)
   v
Prometheus (pull)
Key idea:

OTEL Collector converts OTLP → Prometheus format and exposes it

✅ Step 1: OTEL Collector MUST expose Prometheus endpoint
Your OTEL config needs a Prometheus exporter.

Example (REAL production config)
exporters:
  prometheus:
    endpoint: "0.0.0.0:8889"
This means:

http://otel-collector:8889/metrics
exists.

✅ Step 2: Verify metrics endpoint exists
From inside cluster:

kubectl exec -it otel-collector-0 -n observability -- sh
curl http://localhost:8889/metrics
You should see:

otelcol_process_cpu_seconds_total
otelcol_receiver_accepted_spans
http_server_duration_seconds_bucket
✔️ That means OTEL is working

🧲 Step 3: Tell Prometheus WHERE to scrape
In Kubernetes, Prometheus does NOT scrape random IPs.
It scrapes Services using ServiceMonitor (best practice).

✅ Create Service for OTEL Collector metrics
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: otel-collector-metrics
  namespace: observability
  labels:
    app: otel-collector
spec:
  selector:
    app: otel-collector
  ports:
    - name: metrics
      port: 8889
      targetPort: 8889
Apply:

kubectl apply -f otel-metrics-svc.yaml
✅ Create ServiceMonitor (THIS is the magic)
apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1
kind: ServiceMonitor
metadata:
  name: otel-collector
  namespace: observability
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: otel-collector
  namespaceSelector:
    matchNames:
      - observability
  endpoints:
    - port: metrics
      path: /metrics
      interval: 15s
✔️ Now Prometheus auto-discovers OTEL

🔍 Step 4: Verify in Prometheus UI
Port-forward:

kubectl port-forward svc/prometheus-kube-prometheus-prometheus 9090 -n observability
Check:

Status → Targets

You should see otel-collector as UP

🤯 Important Clarification (Very Common Confusion)
❌ Prometheus does NOT scrape your app directly (in OTEL world)
Instead:

Source	Who exposes metrics
App metrics	OTEL Collector
Infra metrics	Node exporter
K8s metrics	kube-state-metrics
Prometheus scrapes all of them separately.

🆚 Alternative Pattern (Also used in prod)
Direct app scraping (Micrometer style)
App → /actuator/prometheus
Prometheus → scrape app directly
This bypasses OTEL for metrics.

✔️ Simple
❌ No unified pipeline
❌ Less portable

🏆 Recommended Production Approach
Metric Type	Path
App metrics	OTEL → Prometheus
Infra metrics	node-exporter
K8s metrics	kube-state-metrics
🔥 Mental Model (Remember this forever)
OTEL pushes metrics →
OTEL exposes metrics →
Prometheus pulls metrics

🚨 Common Production Issues
Issue	Fix
Target DOWN	Service label mismatch
No metrics	Prometheus exporter missing
Duplicate metrics	App + OTEL both exposing
High memory	Increase batch + limits
🧪 DevOps Debug Checklist
kubectl logs otel-collector -n observability
curl otel-collector.observability.svc.cluster.local:8889/metrics
kubectl get servicemonitor -n observability
