You said:
Please show for very beginners what is used of Service monitor here as a devops engineers I have not heard this term.

ChatGPT said:
Excellent 👏 this is a very important beginner question.

Many DevOps engineers work with Kubernetes but never hear about ServiceMonitor until they install kube-prometheus-stack.

Let’s explain it from zero — very clearly.

🧠 First: How does normal Prometheus work?
In traditional Prometheus (VM setup) you configure scraping like this:

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: "my-app"
    static_configs:
      - targets: ["10.10.1.5:8080"]
Here you manually write:

IP

Port

Path

That’s fine for VMs.

🚨 But in Kubernetes…
Pods:

Get new IPs

Restart frequently

Scale up/down

Move between nodes

So hardcoding IPs is impossible.

🏗️ So what happens in Kubernetes?
Instead of static config, we use:

🔵 Prometheus Operator

When you install:

helm install prometheus prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack
You are NOT installing plain Prometheus.

You are installing:

Prometheus Operator + Prometheus
🤖 What is Prometheus Operator?
It is a Kubernetes controller that:

Watches Kubernetes objects

Automatically generates Prometheus config

Updates scraping without restarting Prometheus

And it introduces new Kubernetes resources like:

ServiceMonitor

PodMonitor

PrometheusRule

🎯 So what is ServiceMonitor?
Simple definition:
A ServiceMonitor tells Prometheus Operator:
“Please scrape this Kubernetes Service.”

That’s it.

It replaces manual scrape_configs.

📦 Think Like This
Without ServiceMonitor:

You manually edit prometheus.yml
With ServiceMonitor:

You create a Kubernetes YAML
Operator updates Prometheus automatically
📊 Visual Comparison
Traditional Prometheus
prometheus.yml
   └── scrape_configs
         └── targets: IP:PORT
Kubernetes + Operator
Service → ServiceMonitor → Prometheus
🔍 What exactly does ServiceMonitor look at?
It selects a Service using labels.

Example:

Your OTEL Service:

metadata:
  labels:
    app: otel-collector
Your ServiceMonitor:

selector:
  matchLabels:
    app: otel-collector
So it means:

“Find all Services with label app=otel-collector and scrape them.”

📦 Full Flow in Your Case
OTEL Pod
   |
   v
Kubernetes Service (otel-collector-metrics)
   |
   v
ServiceMonitor
   |
   v
Prometheus Operator
   |
   v
Prometheus scrapes /metrics
🧪 How to Check If You Are Using Operator
Run:

kubectl get crd | grep monitor
If you see:

servicemonitors.monitoring.coreos.com
Then you are using Prometheus Operator.

🔥 Why DevOps Engineers Love ServiceMonitor
Benefit	Why Important
No manual config edits	GitOps friendly
Auto discovery	Pods scale automatically
Clean separation	Dev team creates Service, DevOps creates ServiceMonitor
Production safe	No Prometheus restarts
🆚 What if I don’t use ServiceMonitor?
Then you must manually edit Prometheus config like:

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: otel
    kubernetes_sd_configs:
      - role: endpoints
Which is:

Harder

Error-prone

Not cloud-native style

🧠 Very Simple Analogy
Think of:

Service → “This is my app”

ServiceMonitor → “Please monitor this app”

Prometheus Operator → “Okay, I will configure Prometheus”

🏁 Final Beginner Summary
ServiceMonitor is:

✔ A Kubernetes object
✔ Used only when Prometheus Operator is installed
✔ Replaces manual scrape config
✔ Used in almost every production K8s cluster

