You said:
So Prometheus is different ,. Prometheus operator is different and serviceMonitor is different resources if yes please show there internal working and please tell me all 3 are pods or different resources of kubernetes????

ChatGPT said:
Very very good question 👏
Now you are thinking like a real DevOps engineer.

Yes ✅ — all three are different things.
And no ❌ — they are NOT all pods.

Let’s break it clearly and visually.

🎯 Big Picture First
When you install:

helm install prometheus prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack
You actually get:

1️⃣ Prometheus (actual metrics server)
2️⃣ Prometheus Operator (controller)
3️⃣ CRDs like ServiceMonitor
These are different components.

🔵 1️⃣ What is Prometheus?
Definition:
Prometheus is the actual monitoring server.

It:

Scrapes metrics

Stores time-series data

Runs queries (PromQL)

In Kubernetes:
It runs as:

Pod
StatefulSet
Service
Check it:

kubectl get pods -n observability
You’ll see something like:

prometheus-kube-prometheus-prometheus-0
So:

Component	Type
Prometheus	Pod (StatefulSet)
🟢 2️⃣ What is Prometheus Operator?
Definition:
Prometheus Operator is a Kubernetes controller.

It:

Watches ServiceMonitor

Watches Prometheus custom resources

Automatically updates Prometheus config

It is like a manager for Prometheus.

In Kubernetes:
It runs as:

Pod (Deployment)
Check it:

kubectl get pods -n observability | grep operator
You may see:

prometheus-operator-xxxx
So:

Component	Type
Prometheus Operator	Pod (Deployment)
🟡 3️⃣ What is ServiceMonitor?
Definition:
ServiceMonitor is NOT a pod.

It is a:

Kubernetes Custom Resource (CRD object)

It is just a YAML object stored in etcd.

It runs nothing.

Check it:

kubectl get servicemonitor -n observability
You’ll see:

otel-collector
But if you run:

kubectl get pods | grep servicemonitor
You will see nothing.

So:

Component	Type
ServiceMonitor	Kubernetes resource (CRD)
🧠 Internal Working (Step by Step)
Let’s see what happens when you create a ServiceMonitor.

Step 1: You apply ServiceMonitor YAML
kubectl apply -f servicemonitor.yaml
It gets stored in:

etcd
Step 2: Prometheus Operator detects it
Operator continuously watches:

servicemonitors.monitoring.coreos.com
Like this (internally):

Watch → Detect new ServiceMonitor → Reconcile
Step 3: Operator updates Prometheus config
Operator dynamically modifies:

/etc/prometheus/config_out/prometheus.env.yaml
Inside Prometheus pod.

It adds new scrape job automatically.

No restart required.

Step 4: Prometheus starts scraping
Now Prometheus:

GET http://service-ip:8889/metrics
Every 15 seconds.

📊 Full Architecture Diagram
You create ServiceMonitor
         ↓
Stored in etcd
         ↓
Prometheus Operator (pod) sees it
         ↓
Operator updates Prometheus config
         ↓
Prometheus (pod) scrapes metrics
🔎 Real Kubernetes Object Types
Let’s summarize clearly:

Component	Kubernetes Type	Is it a Pod?
Prometheus	StatefulSet	✅ Yes
Prometheus Operator	Deployment	✅ Yes
ServiceMonitor	Custom Resource (CRD)	❌ No
PodMonitor	Custom Resource (CRD)	❌ No
PrometheusRule	Custom Resource (CRD)	❌ No
🧠 Simple Analogy
Think like company hierarchy:

Role	Real Example
Prometheus	Worker
Prometheus Operator	Manager
ServiceMonitor	Work instruction
Kubernetes	Company building
🧪 You Can Verify This Yourself
Check CRD:

kubectl get crd servicemonitors.monitoring.coreos.com
Check Operator pod:

kubectl get pods -A | grep operator
Check Prometheus pod:

kubectl get pods -A | grep prometheus
🏁 Final Beginner Understanding
✔ Prometheus = actual metrics server (Pod)
✔ Prometheus Operator = manages Prometheus (Pod)
✔ ServiceMonitor = configuration object (NOT a Pod)

Now you are officially moving from beginner → intermediate DevOps 🚀

