You said:
But if we create new service or pod for new application for Prometheus monitoring then that service labels or pod lables we manually need to update every time in service monitor and pod monitor?

ChatGPT said:
Very very practical question 👏 — this is exactly what happens in real companies.

Short answer:

❌ No, you should NOT manually edit ServiceMonitor / PodMonitor every time you deploy a new app.

If you are doing that → design is wrong.

Let’s understand the correct production approach.

🧠 First Understand How Matching Works
ServiceMonitor works like this:

selector:
  matchLabels:
    monitor: "true"
It does NOT select one specific service name.
It selects all services having that label.

So it is label-based discovery.

🎯 Production Pattern (Very Important)
Instead of:

One ServiceMonitor per application
Companies usually do:

One generic ServiceMonitor
Apps opt-in using labels
✅ Correct Production Approach
Step 1: Create ONE Generic ServiceMonitor
Example:

apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1
kind: ServiceMonitor
metadata:
  name: app-monitor
  namespace: observability
spec:
  namespaceSelector:
    any: true
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      monitoring: enabled
  endpoints:
    - port: metrics
      path: /metrics
      interval: 15s
What this means:

“Scrape ALL services in ALL namespaces that have label monitoring=enabled”

Step 2: When New App Is Deployed
In your application Service YAML:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: payment-service
  namespace: prod
  labels:
    monitoring: enabled
spec:
  ports:
    - name: metrics
      port: 8080
That’s it.

No ServiceMonitor changes needed.

🔁 What Happens Internally?
New Service created
      ↓
Has label monitoring=enabled
      ↓
Prometheus Operator detects match
      ↓
Adds it automatically to scrape config
      ↓
Prometheus starts scraping
Fully automatic.

🏢 How Real Companies Structure This
Dev Team Responsibility:
Add label in Service

Expose /metrics endpoint

DevOps Responsibility:
Maintain generic ServiceMonitor

Maintain Prometheus

Clean separation of duties.

🟢 Same Logic Applies to PodMonitor
Generic PodMonitor:

selector:
  matchLabels:
    monitoring: enabled
Then any pod with:

labels:
  monitoring: enabled
Gets scraped automatically.

🚨 When Would You Create Separate ServiceMonitor?
Only if:

Different scrape interval

Different authentication

Different metrics path

Multi-tenant cluster isolation

Otherwise → one generic monitor is enough.

🔥 What If You Have Multiple Namespaces?
Use:

namespaceSelector:
  any: true
Or:

namespaceSelector:
  matchNames:
    - prod
    - staging
No need to modify again.

🧠 Very Important Concept
Prometheus Operator does:

Label-based dynamic discovery
NOT:

Hardcoded service names
❌ Wrong Beginner Approach
Creating:

service-monitor-payment.yaml
service-monitor-order.yaml
service-monitor-user.yaml
That becomes unmanageable.

✅ Correct Scalable Approach
app-monitor.yaml (single file)
Apps add label to opt-in
That scales to 100+ microservices.

🎯 Final Clear Answer
Do we need to manually update ServiceMonitor every time?

❌ No
✔ Only add proper label in new Service
✔ If using good generic monitor design

You are now thinking in multi-service production scale mindset 👏

