You said:
One simple question if we deploy all observality components using helm then whole deploying our web application we need to do anything changes to work monitoring smoothly of yes please list out all changes

ChatGPT said:
Excellent 👏 this is the correct production question.

You installed:

✅ Prometheus

✅ Grafana

✅ Loki

✅ Tempo

✅ OpenTelemetry Collector

Now question:

When deploying a new web application, do we need to change anything for monitoring to work properly?

✅ Short Answer:
Yes — but only a few small, standard changes.
Nothing complex.

I’ll list everything clearly for a DevOps engineer.

🎯 What You Must Change in Your Web Application Deployment
There are 4 possible areas:

Metrics

Traces

Logs

Kubernetes Labels

🟢 1️⃣ Metrics (Required for Prometheus)
Your app must expose metrics.

Option A — Using OpenTelemetry (recommended modern way)
Add environment variable in Deployment:

env:
  - name: OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT
    value: http://otel-collector.observability.svc.cluster.local:4317
  - name: OTEL_SERVICE_NAME
    value: payment-service
And run Java with agent:

-javaagent:/otel/opentelemetry-javaagent.jar
OR

Option B — Using Spring Boot Micrometer
Expose:

/actuator/prometheus
Add to Service:

ports:
  - name: metrics
    port: 8080
🟢 2️⃣ Add Monitoring Label (VERY IMPORTANT)
If using ServiceMonitor with label matching:

Your Service must include:

metadata:
  labels:
    monitoring: enabled
That’s how Prometheus discovers it.

Without this → it won’t be scraped.

🟢 3️⃣ Traces (For Tempo)
If using OpenTelemetry:

Just set:

env:
  - name: OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT
    value: http://otel-collector.observability.svc.cluster.local:4317
Nothing else required.

If using auto-instrumentation agent → no code changes.

🟢 4️⃣ Logs (For Loki)
If using:

OTEL Collector

Promtail

Grafana Alloy

You don’t need to change application.

Just ensure:

Logs are written to stdout

Example:

System.out.println("Order created");
or

log.info("Order created");
Kubernetes automatically sends container stdout → collector.

🟢 5️⃣ Resource Limits (Production Important)
Add:

resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "200m"
    memory: "256Mi"
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "512Mi"
Because metrics/traces increase memory usage slightly.

🟢 6️⃣ Service Port Naming (Important for ServiceMonitor)
Prometheus matches by port name.

Correct:

ports:
  - name: metrics
    port: 8080
Wrong:

ports:
  - name: http
If ServiceMonitor expects "metrics", names must match.

📦 Minimal Required Changes Summary
When deploying new app, ensure:

Area	Required?	What To Do
Metrics	✅ Yes	Expose endpoint OR use OTEL
Service Label	✅ Yes	Add monitoring=enabled
Traces	If needed	Set OTEL endpoint
Logs	Usually no	Just log to stdout
Resource limits	Recommended	Add CPU/memory limits
🎯 What You DO NOT Need To Do
❌ Do not modify Prometheus
❌ Do not modify ServiceMonitor (if generic setup used)
❌ Do not restart observability stack
❌ Do not manually register app anywhere

Everything works automatically if labels + endpoint are correct.

🏢 Real Production Flow
New microservice deployed:

Dev adds OTEL agent

Dev exposes metrics

Dev adds monitoring label

CI/CD deploys

Prometheus auto-detects

Grafana dashboard auto-shows

Fully automated.

🧠 Final Simple Rule
For smooth monitoring:

Expose metrics
Add label
Send traces to OTEL
Log to stdout

That’s it.

