👉 YES — but not the way you think.

Helm will ALWAYS load:

1️⃣ Chart's internal values.yaml (default)
Then it merges:

2️⃣ values-prod.yaml (because you passed -f)
But it will NOT automatically load your local values.yaml file unless you explicitly pass it.

🔎 Very Important Distinction
There are two different "values.yaml":

1️⃣ Chart default values.yaml
Inside the chart folder (this one is always loaded automatically)

2️⃣ Your custom values.yaml file
This is just another override file — Helm does NOT load it automatically unless you pass:

-f values.yaml
🎯 So In Your Case
If you run:

helm install otel . -f values-prod.yaml
Helm loads:

✔ chart/values.yaml        (always)
✔ values-prod.yaml         (because you passed it)
❌ your local values.yaml  (NOT loaded)
🧠 How Helm Merge Order Works
Priority order (lowest → highest):

Chart default values.yaml
↓
First -f file
↓
Second -f file
↓
--set
Later overrides earlier.

🚀 Production Best Practice
Most teams do:

helm install otel . \
  -f values.yaml \
  -f values-prod.yaml
Meaning:

values.yaml = base config

values-prod.yaml = production overrides

Then prod overrides common settings.

📌 So Your Telemetry Issue?
If telemetry block is still appearing:

It is coming from:

👉 Chart default values.yaml

Because your values-prod.yaml is not overriding it.

✅ How To See Final Merged Values
Run:

helm get values otel -n observability --all
This shows EXACT final merged config.

🏁 Final Clear Statement
If you use only:

-f values-prod.yaml
Then:

✔ Chart default values.yaml is used
✔ values-prod.yaml overrides it
❌ Your other values.yaml is ignored

If you want, paste your values-prod.yaml and I’ll check why 8888 telemetry is still getting injected 🚀


