You said:

\- port: metrics

what is meaning of this?

ChatGPT said:
Good 👌 very important question.

You are asking about this line inside ServiceMonitor:

- port: metrics
Let’s break it clearly for DevOps understanding.

🔎 What Does port: metrics Mean?
It does NOT mean port number.

It means:

👉 Service port name

Kubernetes Services have named ports.

Example from your OpenTelemetry Collector service:

ports:
  - name: metrics
    port: 8889
    targetPort: 8889
Here:

name: metrics ← THIS is what ServiceMonitor uses

port: 8889 ← actual numeric port

🧠 So When You Write:
endpoints:
  - port: metrics
It means:

👉 “Prometheus, scrape the service port whose name is metrics”

NOT:

scrape port 8889
📦 How Prometheus Finds It
Flow:

ServiceMonitor
   ↓
Selects Service by label
   ↓
Looks at service ports
   ↓
Finds port named "metrics"
   ↓
Scrapes its port number (8889)
🔍 Verify In Your Cluster
Run:

kubectl get svc -n observability
Then:

kubectl describe svc <otel-service-name> -n observability
You should see something like:

Port: metrics  8889/TCP
If port name is different (for example http-metrics),
then your ServiceMonitor must use that exact name:

- port: http-metrics
❗ Common Mistake
People write:

- port: 8889   ❌ WRONG
ServiceMonitor does NOT accept number.
It expects port name.

🎯 Simple Analogy
Think like this:

Kubernetes	Real Meaning
name: metrics	Label on the door
port: 8889	Actual room number
ServiceMonitor says:

“Go to the door labeled ‘metrics’”

Not:

“Go to room 8889”

🚀 Final DevOps Rule
Whenever you create ServiceMonitor:

Check Service port name

Use EXACT same name in:

port: <name>
