🔥 STEP 1 — Dockerfile (Very Important)
Your Dockerfile is:

FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jdk
WORKDIR /app
COPY target/job-api-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar app.jar
ENTRYPOINT ["java","-jar","app.jar"]
✅ This is already correct.

You DO NOT need to change Dockerfile.

Because Kubernetes automatically captures:

System.out.println()
Spring logs
Logback logs
As container stdout.

🔥 STEP 2 — Your Application Must Log to Console
If using Spring Boot (default):

In application.yml:

logging:
  level:
    root: INFO
Spring already logs to console.

Example Java log:

private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(JobController.class);

log.info("Job started");
That is enough.

🔥 STEP 3 — Verify Pod Logs First
Deploy app in airflow namespace.

Then:

kubectl logs <your-pod> -n airflow
If logs appear → GOOD.

If not → app logging problem.

🔥 STEP 4 — How Logs Reach Loki
There are 2 common production approaches:

Option A (Recommended in Your Setup)
OpenTelemetry Collector scrapes container logs.

Flow:

Pod stdout → OTel Collector (filelog receiver) → Loki exporter
Option B
Install Promtail (Loki agent)

But since you already use OTel → use Option A.

🔥 STEP 5 — Check OTel Collector Config
Your OTel config must include:

receivers:
  filelog:
    include:
      - /var/log/pods/*/*/*.log
    start_at: beginning

exporters:
  loki:
    endpoint: http://loki.observability:3100/loki/api/v1/push

service:
  pipelines:
    logs:
      receivers: [filelog]
      exporters: [loki]
If this pipeline not defined → logs will NOT go to Loki.

🔥 STEP 6 — How To Verify Loki Receiving Logs
Now answering your main question:

1️⃣ Check Loki Metrics in Prometheus
Run in Prometheus:

loki_ingester_entries_total
If value increasing → Loki receiving logs.

2️⃣ Check OTel Exporter Metrics
otelcol_exporter_sent_log_records
If increasing → OTel sending logs to Loki.

If zero → exporter problem.

3️⃣ Check Loki Logs
kubectl logs -n observability <loki-pod>
Search for errors.

4️⃣ Check in Grafana
Open Grafana:

Explore → Select Loki datasource

Query:

{namespace="airflow"}
If logs visible → SUCCESS.

🔥 Most Common Problem
If nothing appears in Grafana:

❌ OTel not mounted with hostPath
❌ filelog receiver not enabled
❌ Loki exporter wrong endpoint
❌ NetworkPolicy blocking traffic

🎯 Important: Does Your OTel Pod Mount Logs?
Check:

kubectl describe pod <otel-pod> -n observability
Look for volume:

/var/log/pods
If not mounted → OTel cannot read container logs.

