ChatGPT said:
Excellent 👌 now we are going into real production-level logging design.

You’re asking:

How to modify business logic so every application sends structured logs
using OpenTelemetry + logback/log4j2?

Very good question 🔥

Using:

OpenTelemetry

Spring Boot

Logback

Loki

🎯 Goal
Instead of this:

2026-02-16 20:10:01 INFO Job started
We want structured JSON like:

{
  "timestamp": "2026-02-16T20:10:01Z",
  "level": "INFO",
  "service.name": "job-api",
  "trace_id": "abc123",
  "span_id": "xyz789",
  "message": "Job started"
}
That is structured logging.

🔥 STEP 1 — DO NOT change business logic much
Your Java code should stay clean:

private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(JobService.class);

public void processJob() {
    log.info("Job processing started");
}
No JSON writing manually ❌
No traceId manually ❌

We let logging framework handle it.

🔥 STEP 2 — Add OpenTelemetry Java Agent
In Dockerfile:

ENTRYPOINT ["java",
 "-javaagent:/otel-agent.jar",
 "-Dotel.service.name=job-api",
 "-Dotel.logs.exporter=otlp",
 "-Dotel.exporter.otlp.endpoint=http://otel-collector.observability:4317",
 "-jar","app.jar"]
This injects:

traceId

spanId

resource attributes

🔥 STEP 3 — Enable Structured Logging (Logback JSON)
Spring Boot uses Logback by default.

Add dependency:

<dependency>
  <groupId>net.logstash.logback</groupId>
  <artifactId>logstash-logback-encoder</artifactId>
</dependency>
🔥 STEP 4 — Create logback-spring.xml
Create:

src/main/resources/logback-spring.xml
Example:

<configuration>
    <appender name="JSON" class="ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender">
        <encoder class="net.logstash.logback.encoder.LogstashEncoder">
            <includeMdc>true</includeMdc>
            <includeContext>true</includeContext>
        </encoder>
    </appender>

    <root level="INFO">
        <appender-ref ref="JSON"/>
    </root>
</configuration>
Now logs are JSON automatically.

🔥 STEP 5 — Inject TraceId Into Logs (IMPORTANT)
Add this to application.yml:

logging:
  pattern:
    level: "%5p [${spring.application.name:},trace_id=%X{trace_id},span_id=%X{span_id}]"
OR better:

Enable OTel MDC injection:

-Dotel.instrumentation.logback-mdc.enabled=true
Now OpenTelemetry automatically puts:

trace_id

span_id

Into MDC.

🔥 STEP 6 — How It Flows
Business Logic
    ↓
SLF4J logger
    ↓
Logback JSON encoder
    ↓
stdout
    ↓
OTel Agent captures logs
    ↓
OTel Collector (OTLP receiver)
    ↓
Loki
    ↓
Grafana
🔥 STEP 7 — Verify It Works
1️⃣ Check pod logs:

kubectl logs <pod> -n airflow
Should see JSON.

2️⃣ In Prometheus:

otelcol_receiver_accepted_log_records
3️⃣ In Grafana (Loki):

{service_name="job-api"}
Now logs will contain trace_id field.

You can click trace → jump to Tempo.

🔥 That is real observability.

