You said:

net.logstash.logback

this package is not finding by spring.io?

ChatGPT said:
Ahhh good catch 👌🔥

You’re saying:

net.logstash.logback package not found from Spring Initializr (spring.io)

Correct ✅ — because:

net.logstash.logback:logstash-logback-encoder

is NOT a Spring official starter dependency.

It is a third-party logging encoder library.

That’s normal in production projects.

✅ Why It Doesn't Appear in spring.io
When you generate project from:

👉 Spring Initializr

You only see:

Spring Boot starters

Official integrations

But structured logging JSON encoder is external library.

So you must add it manually in pom.xml.

That is standard company practice.

✅ Correct Maven Dependency
Add this inside <dependencies>:

<dependency>
    <groupId>net.logstash.logback</groupId>
    <artifactId>logstash-logback-encoder</artifactId>
    <version>7.4</version>
</dependency>
⚠️ Always specify version.

Spring Initializr does not auto-manage this dependency.

🔍 Verify It Is Valid
This library:

Is widely used in enterprise

Used for JSON structured logging

Works with Logback (default in Spring Boot)

It works perfectly with:

👉 Logback

🏢 Is This Company Standard?
Yes ✅

Most companies use one of:

1️⃣ logstash-logback-encoder (very common)
2️⃣ log4j2 JSON layout
3️⃣ OpenTelemetry log exporter (newer approach)

Your approach = industry standard.

🚀 Alternative (If You Want Cleaner Setup)
Spring Boot 3.2+ also supports:

logging.json.enabled=true
But that does NOT include trace_id automatically.

So for:

✔ structured logs
✔ trace correlation
✔ Loki compatibility

logstash-logback-encoder is still best.

🎯 Final Answer
It does not appear in spring.io because:

It is not a Spring starter

It is third-party encoder

You must manually add dependency

That is normal in enterprise apps.

